1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,117
One move.

2
00:00:01,117 --> 00:00:02,217
Two choices.

3
00:00:02,217 --> 00:00:10,550
Would you move your pawn closer to the finish, or place a wall to slow

4
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:12,217
your opponent down?

5
00:00:12,217 --> 00:00:15,000
Take a second to decide.

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,083
Moving looks like progress.

7
00:00:17,083 --> 00:00:21,250
But a wall can change both players’ routes.

8
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:25,417
The better choice depends on what happens next.

9
00:00:25,417 --> 00:00:33,233
That is the challenge behind our proposal: an AI opponent for Quoridor that makes decisions

10
00:00:33,233 --> 00:00:40,000
under a time limit, while keeping the game enjoyable for a human player.

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,783
We are Team 26.

12
00:00:41,783 --> 00:00:48,450
Our proposal brings together a rules-correct game, a Monte Carlo Tree Search opponent, selectable difficulty,

13
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:50,667
and a clear graphical interface.

14
00:00:50,667 --> 00:00:57,333
We would also deliver software design documentation and a user manual, so someone else can

15
00:00:57,333 --> 00:01:00,000
understand and run the finished game.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,933
Team skills — pending confirmation.

17
00:01:08,933 --> 00:01:25,000
Replace this section after the team confirms its experience.

18
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Quoridor has a clear goal: reach the opposite edge.

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,333
Each turn offers movement or wall placement, but both players must retain a route to

20
00:01:38,333 --> 00:01:39,450
their goal.

21
00:01:39,450 --> 00:01:43,883
This creates strategic choices without a real-time interface.

22
00:01:43,883 --> 00:01:52,217
Board Game Arena provides a digital reference; our focus would be a documented MCTS opponent

23
00:01:52,217 --> 00:01:55,000
with difficulty we can evaluate.

24
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,883
So, how would the AI choose?

25
00:01:58,883 --> 00:02:04,717
MCTS builds a search tree from the current position.

26
00:02:04,717 --> 00:02:14,450
It selects a promising branch, expands a possible move, simulates what might happen, and sends

27
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:18,333
the result back through the tree.

28
00:02:18,333 --> 00:02:28,050
Repeating this process helps it balance exploring new options with investigating moves that have performed

29
00:02:28,050 --> 00:02:30,000
well so far.

30
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,867
We would start with standard MCTS, then compare random rollouts with simple route-aware rollouts.

31
00:02:36,867 --> 00:02:42,250
Long simulations would have a cutoff and a documented path-distance estimate.

32
00:02:42,250 --> 00:02:49,600
Wall placement creates a large branching factor, so we would profile the search and evaluate

33
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:51,567
candidate filtering if necessary.

34
00:02:51,567 --> 00:02:55,000
The underlying game rules would remain unchanged.

35
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,617
The AI needs a reliable game underneath it.

36
00:02:59,617 --> 00:03:08,267
Our rules module would handle legal pawn movement, wall conflicts, state transitions, and victory detection.

37
00:03:08,267 --> 00:03:16,917
A graph-based check would reject any wall that removes either player’s route to their goal.

38
00:03:16,917 --> 00:03:25,000
Separating the rules from search and presentation would let us test these behaviors independently.

39
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:32,367
We propose Java and JavaFX, with Maven for builds and JUnit for tests.

40
00:03:32,367 --> 00:03:37,450
JavaFX suits the board interface and its visual feedback.

41
00:03:37,450 --> 00:03:45,950
The AI would search copied game states in a background task with a bounded time

42
00:03:45,950 --> 00:03:46,517
budget.

43
00:03:46,517 --> 00:03:55,000
That separation should keep the interface responsive and prevent search from modifying the live board.

44
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:03,650
From the player’s perspective, the important questions are simple: what can I do, what just

45
00:04:03,650 --> 00:04:07,700
happened, and how difficult is my opponent?

46
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:16,350
Our proposed interface would answer those through legal-move feedback, wall previews, and clear difficulty controls.

47
00:04:16,350 --> 00:04:19,800
An unbeatable AI might be impressive.

48
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:25,000
An opponent you enjoy playing against is our goal.

49
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:31,600
Difficulty would initially use search budgets of point two, point five, and one second.

50
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:35,383
Those are starting values, not proven difficulty levels.

51
00:04:35,383 --> 00:04:40,567
We would adjust them using measured playing strength and response time.

52
00:04:40,567 --> 00:04:47,633
Giving the AI more time is useful only if the result creates a meaningful and

53
00:04:47,633 --> 00:04:50,000
usable difference for the player.

54
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:58,033
We would compare difficulty levels and a movement-only shortest-path baseline, initially using one hundred games

55
00:04:58,033 --> 00:05:01,250
per matchup with balanced first-player assignments.

56
00:05:01,250 --> 00:05:07,150
We would report win rates, response times, and unfinished games separately.

57
00:05:07,150 --> 00:05:14,650
Rules and integration tests would cover jumps, blocked paths, wall conflicts, restart, and victory.

58
00:05:14,650 --> 00:05:20,000
This would give us evidence to explain our design decisions.

59
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:29,050
Our provisional milestones are a playable rules prototype by November, an MCTS baseline by December,

60
00:05:29,050 --> 00:05:38,100
difficulty evaluation by February, and an integrated, tested game with documentation by April, ready for

61
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:39,917
the May showcase.

62
00:05:39,917 --> 00:05:47,150
We would align these dates with module deadlines and the supervisor’s advice.

63
00:05:47,150 --> 00:05:55,000
Each iteration would produce something demonstrable, rather than leaving integration until the end.

64
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:02,217
We propose paired ownership across rules, AI, GUI, and testing and documentation, with a lead

65
00:06:02,217 --> 00:06:04,617
and reviewer for each area.

66
00:06:04,617 --> 00:06:07,983
Assignments would follow the team’s confirmed skills.

67
00:06:07,983 --> 00:06:14,717
Weekly meetings, a shared backlog, and reviewed pull requests would help us coordinate work.

68
00:06:14,717 --> 00:06:20,000
Design notes and the user manual would develop alongside the software.

69
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:27,983
The main risks are limited MCTS experience, expensive wall validation, weak rollouts, and integration problems

70
00:06:27,983 --> 00:06:29,567
alongside other coursework.

71
00:06:29,567 --> 00:06:37,550
We would address these through early prototypes, profiling, bounded search budgets, paired learning, and regular

72
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:38,083
integration.

73
00:06:38,083 --> 00:06:45,000
Our priority would be a correct, usable two-player game, before adding optional features.

74
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:51,950
We would check code and asset licenses, avoid unauthorized branding, and design the game without

75
00:06:51,950 --> 00:06:54,267
player accounts or personal-data collection.

76
00:06:54,267 --> 00:06:57,967
Clear feedback and accessible controls would support usability.

77
00:06:57,967 --> 00:07:04,900
Any human playtesting would first be discussed with the supervisor for consent and ethics requirements.

78
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:10,000
We would also welcome guidance on search evaluation and game complexity.

79
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,417
Back to our opening question: move, or place a wall?

80
00:07:14,417 --> 00:07:18,817
We do not want to guess which AI is better.

81
00:07:18,817 --> 00:07:25,433
We want to build an opponent whose decisions, difficulty, and limitations we can test and

82
00:07:25,433 --> 00:07:25,883
explain.

83
00:07:25,883 --> 00:07:32,500
Choose Team 26 for a clear plan: a rules-correct game, a responsive interface, and an

84
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:33,817
evaluated MCTS opponent.

85
00:07:33,817 --> 00:07:39,117
Our goal is an opponent that makes you think one move further.

86
00:07:39,117 --> 00:07:40,000
Thank you.
